        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Under $500 the useful question is not which treadmill is best. It is which compromises
          you can live with — because at this price every machine has made the same three, and
          pretending otherwise is how people end up disappointed six weeks in.
        </p>

        <div id="what-you-cannot-get" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. What $500 Cannot Buy, Stated Plainly</h2>
<p>
            Three things are effectively unavailable at this price, and knowing that up front
            makes the rest of the decision straightforward. (For the tier below, see the
            <a href="/best-treadmills-under-300/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills under $300</a>.)
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A running deck.</strong> Decks at this price run 40 to 50 inches. A 60-inch
            deck is the running specification and it does not appear below roughly $900. If you
            intend to run, this is the wrong budget, and no amount of shopping will change that —
            our <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running guide</a>
            starts where this one ends.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A motor rated for sustained work.</strong> Continuous-duty ratings here are
            typically 1.5 to 2.25 CHP. That is adequate for walking and marginal for jogging, and
            the peak-horsepower figures on the box — often 4.0 or higher — describe a burst the
            motor cannot hold for more than a few seconds. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains the distinction.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>A frame that does not move.</strong> Assembled weights are 60 to 130 lbs
            against 250 lbs and up for mid-range machines. Light frames flex, and above a brisk
            walk you will feel it.
          </p>
          <p>
            What you can get is a genuinely useful walking machine, and for a great many people
            that is exactly the right purchase. Most treadmills are used at 2.5 to 4 mph
            regardless of what they cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="two-categories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Two Different Machines at the Same Price</h2>
<p>
            This budget contains two distinct product categories that are rarely presented as
            alternatives, and choosing the right one matters more than choosing between models.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Walking pads</strong> are deck-only machines with no upright and no handrails.
            They are 4 to 6 inches tall, 60 to 90 lbs, slide under a bed or a sofa, and top out
            around 4 mph. Because the whole product is a deck and a motor, the money goes into
            those rather than into a frame, which is why a $400 <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>walking pad</a> often has a better
            belt and a quieter motor than a $400 folding treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Budget folding treadmills</strong> have an upright, a console, handrails and
            usually some incline. They look like a proper treadmill and, at this price, are
            spread thin: the same money is buying twice as much structure, so the deck, motor and
            frame are all weaker than the walking pad equivalent.
          </p>
          <p>
            The decision comes down to whether you need handrails and incline. If you want
            support while walking, or you want incline to raise effort without raising impact,
            you need the folding treadmill and you accept a weaker machine for it. If you simply
            want to walk, the walking pad is the better product at this price, and it is not close.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="specs-to-check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. The Four Specifications Worth Checking</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Continuous-duty horsepower, not peak.</strong> Look for 2.0 CHP or better if
            you will jog at all; 1.5 CHP is walking only. If a listing quotes only peak
            horsepower, assume the continuous figure is unflattering, because it would be quoted
            if it were not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight capacity, discounted.</strong> Stated capacities here cluster around
            220 to 265 lbs, and as elsewhere the figure is a warranty boundary rather than a
            comfortable operating point. Treat it as 50 lbs lower than printed. Our
            <a href="/best-treadmills-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">high-capacity guide</a>
            explains why.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Deck length against your stride.</strong> Forty inches suits walking. Fifty
            inches allows light jogging for a shorter user. Under 45 inches, jogging is not
            realistic for most adults regardless of what the top speed says.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Warranty length, and what it covers.</strong> This tier ranges from a
            dismissive 90 days on parts to a genuinely reasonable two years, and that spread tells
            you more about a manufacturer's confidence than any specification does. It is the
            single most useful comparison point at this price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. How We Chose</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines, and nothing on this page should be read as if we
            had. Because models at this price change so often, each pick stands for a category as
            much as a product, chosen on the specifications that decide whether a budget machine is
            worth having: continuous-duty motor rating, deck size, assembled weight, weight
            capacity and warranty terms.
          </p>
          <p>
            Of those, the continuous motor rating matters most. A 1.5 to 2.25 CHP motor is a
            light-duty rating, and on a walking machine it spends much of each session close to
            its working limit. That is not a defect — these motors are doing exactly what they
            were built to do — but it is why heat and belt friction matter more here than at any
            other price.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is also why the maintenance advice at the end of this page is not optional. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-reviews-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">standard methodology</a>
            explains how we assess machines from published data.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Six Best Machines Under $500</h2>
<p>
            Two of these are walking pads rather than treadmills, and they are here because at
            this price they are frequently the better product. All six are specific machines sold on
            Amazon US. A manufacturer-refurbished machine, a buying route rather than a model, is the
            other option worth watching; the used-market section below covers it. Each entry says who it is for.
          </p>
          <p>
            One machine that is no longer an option deserves a mention, because it is still what
            people search for at this budget: the
            <a href="/weslo-cadence-g-5-9-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Weslo Cadence G 5.9</a>
            has been discontinued. Its review covers what a used one is worth and why a walking pad
            now does that job better.
          </p>
        </div>
