        <!-- Closing CTA -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Under $500 you are buying a walking machine, and a
            <strong class="text-white">walking pad</strong> is usually the best version of one
            because none of your money goes into a frame you would not use. Add a rail only if
            you want support; add incline only if you will use it.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If you might jog, want real incline, or weigh over about 220 lbs, save for the
            <a href="/best-treadmill-under-1000-reviews/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">$800-$1,000 tier</a>
            or buy <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">used</a>.
            A machine that cannot do what you need is expensive at any price.
          </p>
